1567.1,Yahya related to me from Malik that Yahya ibn Said heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mention that a face wound in which the bone was bared was like a head wound in which the bone was bared
1567.2,Yahya related to me from Malik that Yahya ibn Said heard Sulayman ibn Yasar mention that a face wound in which the bone was bared was like a head wound in which the bone was bared
1568.1,Yahya related to me from Malik from Yahya ibn Said
1568.2,"Malik related to me from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that a man
in the time of Aban ibn Uthman's amirate freed all of his slaves and
did not have other property than them. Aban ibn Uthman took charge of
the slaves and they were divided into three groups. Then he drew lots
on the basis that which ever group drew the dead man's arrow would be
free. The arrow fell to one of the thirds, and that third was freed."
1569.1,
1569.2,"Yahya related to me from Malik, from Yahya ibn Said, that it reached him that Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, Salim ibn Abdullah, and Urwah ibn Az-Zubayr used to say: If a runaway slave steals something for which the prescribed punishment is amputation, he is to be amputated."
1570.1,Yahya related to me from Malik from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr allowed retaliation for a head wound which splintered the bone
1570.2,Yahya related to me from Malik from Rabia ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman that Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr allowed retaliation for a head wound which splintered the bone
1571.1,"Yahya related to me from Malik, that it reached him that a slave girl was brought to Umar ibn Al-Khattab, whose master had burned her with fire or injured her with it, and he freed her. Malik said: The established practice among us is that the emancipation of a man who has debts that encompass his wealth is not valid, and the emancipation of a boy is not valid until he reaches puberty or the age of puberty. And the emancipation of one under guardianship of his wealth is not valid, even if he reaches puberty, until he manages his own wealth."
1571.2,"Yahya related to me from Malik, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd ar-Rahman, that Az-Zubayr ibn Al-Awwam met a man who had apprehended a thief and intended to take him to the ruler. Az-Zubayr interceded for him to let him go. The man said: No, until I take him to the ruler. Az-Zubayr said: When you take him to the ruler, may Allah curse the interceder and the one for whom intercession is made."
